WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:03.340
A peasant heel in hand knitting refers

2
00:00:03.370 --> 00:00:06.060
to a sock heel that is
shaped with decreases.

3
00:00:06.090 --> 00:00:11.380
Instead of short rows. It is knit
in the round. For machine knitters,

4
00:00:11.410 --> 00:00:13.460
shaping on the ribber is do-able

5
00:00:13.490 --> 00:00:17.860
but tedious,
and here's a single bed faux peasant heel

6
00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:21.940
that's a combination
of decreases and sew-as-you-go.

7
00:00:21.970 --> 00:00:26.500
For this sample, I used a cast
on rag and a row of ravel cord.

8
00:00:26.530 --> 00:00:31.460
Casting on the number of heel stitches
in my pattern, I knit one row

9
00:00:31.490 --> 00:00:37.660
before knitting back,
I hung a bobby pin over the working yarn.

10
00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:40.820
Then, working my decreases on both sides,

11
00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:45.860
I used a two-step decrease
three stitches from the edge.

12
00:00:45.890 --> 00:00:51.460
After the decreases are done,
I held the pin so the yarn stays tight,

13
00:00:51.490 --> 00:00:56.620
and the pin isn't picked up by the
knitting carriage and knit to the right.

14
00:00:56.650 --> 00:01:02.860
Before knitting back, I hung another
bobby pin on the working yarn.

15
00:01:02.890 --> 00:01:05.130
Holding the pin to keep the thread tight

16
00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:08.930
and the pin away from the carriage,
I knit back.

17
00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:13.100
This sequence is repeated,
decreasing every other row for the number

18
00:01:13.130 --> 00:01:18.570
of decreases indicated in the pattern.
On the carriage side, hang a pin,

19
00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:25.540
work the decreases, knit across,
hang a pin and knit back.

20
00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:29.740
When the decreases are complete,
I'll start the sew-as-you-go to form

21
00:01:29.770 --> 00:01:34.260
the heel and toe pocket.
Opposite the carriage,

22
00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:39.930
hang the loop being held by the pin
on an empty needle, knit across.

23
00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:45.380
Opposite the carriage, hang the loop
on the next empty needle and knit across.

24
00:01:45.410 --> 00:01:50.340
Keep repeating this until the original
number of needles are back in work.

25
00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:55.850
The decreased side looks like the hand knit
version, and the sew-as-you-go technique

26
00:01:55.880 --> 00:01:59.200
makes the shaping go fast
with no Kitchener stitch.

